WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:00.986
rvoip

00:00:01.105 --> 00:00:02.024
a complete VoIP

00:00:01.836 --> 00:00:02.850
stack in Rust

00:00:02.708 --> 00:00:03.663
from SIP

00:00:04.182 --> 00:00:04.948
rvoip is a

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:06.021
Rust library that

00:00:05.833 --> 00:00:06.953
bundles SIP

00:00:06.977 --> 00:00:08.710
RTP WebRTC

00:00:08.793 --> 00:00:10.915
QUIC / WebTransport

00:00:11.045 --> 00:00:12.696
Media over QUIC

00:00:12.507 --> 00:00:13.910
and voice-AI agents

00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:14.795
behind one call

00:00:14.607 --> 00:00:15.160
model

00:00:15.441 --> 00:00:16.207
so a call

00:00:16.019 --> 00:00:16.773
goes from your

00:00:16.585 --> 00:00:17.245
code to the

00:00:17.057 --> 00:00:17.964
wire without a

00:00:17.776 --> 00:00:19.367
separate SIP proxy

00:00:19.894 --> 00:00:20.731
On a single

00:00:20.543 --> 00:00:23.408
16-vCPU AWS c8i

00:00:23.219 --> 00:00:23.714
.

00:00:23.526 --> 00:00:25.103
4xlarge one rvoip

00:00:24.915 --> 00:00:26.294
process handled about

00:00:26.165 --> 00:00:27.945
8,300 calls per

00:00:27.757 --> 00:00:28.959
second of SIP

00:00:28.806 --> 00:00:30.410
signalling over TLS

00:00:30.339 --> 00:00:31.341
or UDP

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:33.322
and about 1,100

00:00:33.134 --> 00:00:35.127
to 1,200 calls

00:00:34.938 --> 00:00:36.011
per second with

00:00:35.846 --> 00:00:36.636
G .

00:00:36.448 --> 00:00:37.686
711 media

00:00:38.198 --> 00:00:39.353
Full method and

00:00:39.165 --> 00:00:40.155
per-trial data

00:00:40.297 --> 00:00:42.690
RFC 7501 /

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.002
7502 benchmark report

00:00:45.534 --> 00:00:46.548
Full story on

00:00:46.359 --> 00:00:47.255
the dashboard
